            <ArticleTitle>Primary suprasellar malignant tumor with odontogenic features: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E380-2; discussion E382</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1227/01.NEU.0000349923.24247.D1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Primary malignant odontogenic tumors in the suprasellar region have rarely been reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A partially cystic tumor that originated from the stalk and showed rapid enlargement was observed in a 56-year-old woman.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Gross total removal of the tumor was performed with sacrifice of the stalk. The tumor was a malignant epithelial type with odontogenic features, whereas findings consistent with craniopharyngioma were absent. The tumor recurred 14 months after surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The present unusual tumor may represent a novel member of malignant tumors of the suprasellar region. Given the common embryological origin of craniopharyngiomas to odontogenic lesions, a possible correlation with craniopharyngioma was suggested.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
